    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the measure of the smallest interior angle, we start by understanding that the sum of the interior angles of a triangle is always \\(180^\\circ\\). Given that the interior angles are in the ratio \\(5:6:7\\), we can represent the angles as \\(5x\\), \\(6x\\), and \\(7x\\), where \\(x\\) is a common multiplier.\n\nFirst, we set up the equation that represents the sum of the interior angles of the triangle:\n\\[\n5x + 6x + 7x = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNext, we combine the terms on the left side of the equation:\n\\[\n18x = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTo find the value of \\(x\\), we divide both sides of the equation by 18:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{180^\\circ}{18} = 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow that we have the value of \\(x\\), we can find the measure of each angle. The smallest angle is \\(5x\\):\n\\[\n5x = 5 \\times 10^\\circ = 50^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of the smallest interior angle is \\(\\boxed{50}\\).",
